 much more  moderate communist but by this point  many people were simply sick and tired  of the broader regime an urban a class  it for a whole five days he did however  in the communist party's monopoly on  power before resigning after several  weeks of negotiations a compromise  government was established which for the  first time since 1948 was majority  non-communist Havel was made president  of Czechoslovakia by this government on  the 28th December and soon after this he  gave a blanket pardon to all of  Czechoslovakia is political prisoners  minor reforms were undertaken but the  process of formally ending communism and  transitioning to a capitalist model  needed to wait for this new government  to gain legitimacy this was done via  elections in June of 1990 we saw have L  retained the presidency central  parliamentary elections were vows  the Civic Forum win which then ruled  alongside its Slovak equivalent the  public against violence party the  separation of the anti communist bloc  into Czech and Slovak parts is  indicative of a brand new problem that  Czechoslovakia now faced the resurgence  of nationalism which communism had  previously suppressed this doesn't mean  that this nationalism couldn't be dealt  with the majority of Czech and Slovak  citizens along with their politicians  were in favor of maintaining the  Czechoslovakian union a new election in  June 1992 saw two new frontrunners the  Civic Democratic Party led by vat Slav  Klaus and the movement for a democratic  Slovakia led by Vladimir metzia amid  1992 Klaus and Metz er sat down to  discuss what the new Czechoslovakia  would look like and they couldn't agree  on anything  Mitsy I wanted the slovak half of the  country to have its own currency and  more autonomy than the Czechs wanted to  give it you should be aware that met  CEA's demands were largely his own and  that slovak politicians would have been  quite happy to simply approve of a union  of equals instead he made it clear that  